WEBVTT

NOTE week12-clip3-shortcuts
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.613 line:2 align:center
A model can reach a high score
by reading the wrong thing.

2
00:00:03.613 --> 00:00:06.645 line:2 align:center
The score tells you the
label came out right.

3
00:00:06.645 --> 00:00:11.083 line:2 align:center
It does not tell you which
part of the image produced it.

4
00:00:11.283 --> 00:00:14.240 line:2 align:center
DeGrave and colleagues built two datasets.

5
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.392 line:2 align:center
The first takes positives
from GitHub-COVID

6
00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:20.664 line:2 align:center
and negatives from ChestX-ray14,

7
00:00:20.664 --> 00:00:24.274 line:2 align:center
so source and label
line up almost exactly.

8
00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:29.504 line:2 align:center
The second, from BIMCV and PadChest,
is one region and one research team:

9
00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:31.750 line:2 align:center
the favorable case.

10
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.910 line:2 align:center
Saliency maps and generative

11
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.841 line:2 align:center
counterfactuals showed
what the models used:

12
00:00:36.841 --> 00:00:41.614 line:2 align:center
laterality markers, arrows and
text found only in one archive,

13
00:00:41.614 --> 00:00:45.973 line:2 align:center
the radiopacity of the image
borders, and patient positioning.

14
00:00:45.973 --> 00:00:48.746 line:2 align:center
Moving the clavicle
region to the top border

15
00:00:48.746 --> 00:00:52.817 line:2 align:center
raised the predicted odds of COVID 19.

16
00:00:53.017 --> 00:00:55.662 line:2 align:center
On radiographs from a new hospital system,

17
00:00:55.662 --> 00:01:00.608 line:2 align:center
half of the predictive performance
was lost. The authors concluded

18
00:01:00.608 --> 00:01:05.739 line:2 align:center
that only a minority of it was
ever genuine COVID 19 pathology.

19
00:01:05.739 --> 00:01:09.350 line:2 align:center
The rest was the source, not the disease.

20
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:15.309 line:2 align:center
Here is the part that catches people
out. Some shortcuts do not transfer,

21
00:01:15.309 --> 00:01:19.827 line:2 align:center
so the score falls and you see it.
Others, like patient positioning,

22
00:01:19.827 --> 00:01:22.191 line:2 align:center
are consistent in both datasets:

23
00:01:22.191 --> 00:01:27.398 line:2 align:center
the score holds up and the model
is still not reading the lungs.

24
00:01:27.398 --> 00:01:31.150 line:2 align:center
External validation is
necessary, not sufficient.

25
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.827 line:2 align:center
An average hides the group
a model works worst on.

26
00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:39.872 line:2 align:center
Sensitivity of 0.85 overall can be 0.90 in

27
00:01:39.872 --> 00:01:45.617 line:2 align:center
80% of patients and 0.65 in the other 20.

28
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:50.718 line:2 align:center
Report subgroups with intervals, validate
externally, look at what the model uses,

29
00:01:50.718 --> 00:01:55.580 line:2 align:center
and test whether the site can
be predicted from its features.
